f self.usesTime(): record.asctime = ...\ns = self.formatMessage(record)\nif record.exc_info: ... record.exc_text = ...\nif record.exc_text: ...\nif record.stack_info: ...\n```\nSo record.message is overwritten each time format is called. So setting record.message is harmless-ish but let's avoid mutating shared record where possible.\n\nNote requirement 3: \"用 Python 的 len 和字符串切片计数\" and truncation replaces middle. Also \"不包含...异常回溯\" — super().format(record) appends exception text after message. Since we only replace record.message occurrences within msg, exception text unaffected. Good.\n\nBut careful: if the message body appears in the exception text too... edge.\n\nAlso multi-line prefix logic uses `record.message` — if we truncate, multi-line alignment uses old record.message. Hmm. The truncation might add/remove newlines? No — same content portions, newlines preserved in the kept parts. But the alignment replace triggers on \"\\n\" in msg and finds prefix via msg.find(record.message) — if record.message no longer a substring of msg (because message truncated in msg), find returns -1 → prefix = msg[:-1]... bad. So order matters: do newline alignment first, then truncation? But then truncation would break after alignment — replacing record.message in aligned msg... record.message still original with newlines; in aligned msg the message body has \"\\n\"+prefix inserted, so replace fails.\n\nBest approach: compute truncated message text first, then build msg using formatMessage with truncated message. Cleanest: override format() to temporarily set record.message? Actually approach: \n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    original_message = record.message if hasattr(record, 'message') else None  # not set yet first time\n```\n\nHmm. Let me think about how to do this robustly:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    keep_full = getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True\n    limit = self.max_message_chars\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    truncated = None\n    if limit is not None and not keep_full and len(message) > limit:\n        truncated = _truncate(message, limit)\n    if truncated is not None:\n        # temporarily substitute\n        saved_args = record.args\n        record.args = None\n        record.message = truncated ... \n```\nGetting complicated. Alternative: after super().format, do:\n\n```python\nmsg = super().format(record)\nfull = record.message\nif truncated is not None:\n    # replace in formatted text\n    msg = msg.replace(full, truncated)\n```\nBut the newline alignment part uses record.message against msg — do alignment before replacement? Order: super().format gives msg containing full message + exc text. We want final: formatted with truncated message, with newline alignment applied to prefix of truncated message.\n\nSimplest: build msg, apply truncation replacement first for the message body only, then do newline alignment using the truncated text as the \"message\" search key.\n\nSo:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    message = record.message\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True and message:\n        if len(message) > self.max_message_chars:\n            truncated = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n            msg = msg.replace(message, truncated, 1)  # hmm message appears once at msg body start typically\n            message = truncated\n    if \"\\n\" in msg and message:\n        idx = msg.find(message)\n        if idx > 0:\n            prefix = msg[:idx]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n    ...\n```\n\nWait original code: `if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message: prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]`. It finds message in msg. If message occurs at position where prefix computed. With original behavior, if find returns -1, prefix = msg[:-1] — weird but original. My version guards idx>0 which changes behavior for idx==0 (prefix=\"\" → \"\\n\" unchanged, same result as msg.replace(\"\\n\",\"\\n\")) and idx==-1 (original gives weird prefix; mine skips). Slight behavior change but acceptable? Safer to preserve exactly: use `msg.find(message)` and keep prefix logic. If find == -1, prefix = msg[:-1] → that's original behaviour too... original with record.message = full message would find it. Since I replaced with truncated, find should work.\n\nActually let me keep near-original:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        message = record.message\n        limit = self.max_message_chars\n        if limit is not None and message and len(message) > limit and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            truncated = _truncate_message(message, limit)\n            idx = msg.find(message)\n            if idx >= 0:\n                msg = msg[:idx] + truncated + msg[idx + len(message):]\n            message = truncated\n        # 快速路径：单行日志（绝大多数情况）无需做对齐处理\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm if message not found (idx<0) we skip truncation but then message=truncated variable not updated... In my code if idx<0 msg unchanged and message stays original? No, I set message = truncated outside the if. Let's be careful: if idx<0 we shouldn't truncate at all. Rare. Let me write:\n\n```python\n        if (\n            limit is not None\n            and message\n            and len(message) > limit\n            and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True\n        ):\n            truncated = _truncate_message(message, limit)\n            idx = msg.find(message)\n            if idx >= 0:\n                msg = msg[:idx] + truncated + msg[idx + len(message) :]\n                message = truncated\n```\n\nThen alignment uses `message`. But alignment should use record.message per original when no truncation — same variable. OK.\n\nCareful with multi-line + truncation: truncated message may still contain \"\\n\" — fine.\n\nNow `_truncate_message(message, limit)`:\n```\n_MESSAGE_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, limit: int) -> str:\n    marker = _MESSAGE_OMITTED_MARKER\n    remaining = limit - len(marker)  # 32-15=17\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2  # 9\n    tail = remaining - head  # 8\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\nWith limit=32: 9+15+8=32 ✓.\n\nDefault init: init_logger/_setup_logger reads envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS. logger.py imports `from deepinfer import envs`? Check whether it imports envs — need to check the file. Lines 1-40 imports did not include envs. Need to add import. But careful about circular import: environ.py imports only stdlib. fine.\n\nFormatting spec says \"日志模块只扩展格式化器和默认初始化，可增加必要标准库导入或辅助函数\". Importing deepinfer.envs is not a stdlib import... but necessary. Reading config in _setup_logger requires envs. Alternatively import inside function to avoid cycle. I'll do `from deepinfer import envs` at module level? Hmm risk of circular imports: deepinfer/__init__.py may import logger etc. Safer: local import inside _setup_logger.\n\nBut test may call logger._setup_logger() after rearm, and expects new formatter with cached value. Also \"重复初始化不能重复添加处理器\" — existing code handles that.\n\nAlso there might be a test that checks environ config triggers ValueError when invalid — that occurs at _setup_logger call time when env set invalid. Fine.\n\nNow for stats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nNow let me check the tail of logger.py to confirm structure and whether there are other things after init_logger. Read lines 100-200.\n\nAlso check `get_logger` function mentioned in the table (\"获取日志对象的函数\" = init_logger). Fine.\n\nLet's read remaining logger lines to confirm nothi